Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables, STRUCTURE.md and the
tools) are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

The legal texts are official texts of the State of Libya (laws, decisions, regulations, constitutional texts,
agreements). The sources and their own terms:

1. Ministry of Justice of Libya (https://aladel.gov.ly): the Civil Code and Law No. 6 of 2016, read from the text layer
   of the Ministry's PDF (retrieved 29 October 2026). The site shows only a general "all rights reserved" footer and
   states no terms of reuse for its legal texts.
   Changes by Lexifina: the text layer was converted to Markdown, one file per article; Arabic presentation forms and
   a few look-alike letters were converted to standard Arabic letters, wrapped lines were joined, page numbers and
   footnotes were left out. The wording of the law was not changed.

2. DCAF, Geneva Centre for Security Sector Governance, Libya Security Sector Legal Database
   (https://security-legislation.ly), Arabic texts (retrieved from 26 November 2026). The site carries the footer
   "Copyright (c) 2024 DCAF. All rights reserved." and states no other terms of reuse. DCAF marks its texts as
   "un-official transcription" and refers to the original as published by the Libyan authorities; every commit taken
   from it carries "Source-status: unofficial transcription". Lexifina takes the legal text only (no DCAF
   translation, notes or layout).
   Changes by Lexifina: HTML converted to Markdown, one file per article; whitespace squeezed. The wording was not
   changed.

Neither the Ministry of Justice nor DCAF endorses this repository. For anything that matters, read the original as
published in the Official Gazette of Libya.
